From ec1c5cfba1a5f608f3625c5741870a9de58e3dfd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 19:14:52 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 07/15] vieww-audio-plugin: CLAP and VST3 wrappers and hosts,
 validated against both formats

A new crate: write one Plugin (a vieww-audio Parametric plus process()),
export it with export_clap!/export_vst3!, or host .clap/.vst3 modules.
No SDK or bindings dependency: the ABIs are transcribed from the CLAP and
VST3 headers (MIT) in sys modules, with layout tests whose numbers come
from C/C++ programs compiled against those headers.

- Shared Driver: sample-accurate parameter automation by splitting blocks
  at every change, preallocated event queues, flush-to-zero/DAZ guard.
- CLAP: entry, factory, plugin, params (with host rescan on state load),
  state, audio-ports, note-ports (CLAP + MIDI dialects), latency (host
  notified on change), tail; atomics mirror values for the main thread and
  the audio thread only try_locks; aliased in-place buffers are copied.
- VST3: single-component objects implementing IComponent,
  IAudioProcessor, IEditController, IProcessContextRequirements and
  IMidiMapping; normalised values through the plugin's tapers; hidden
  per-channel CC/pressure/bend parameters turned back into MIDI events;
  note-expression note-id tracking; COM and plain UID byte orders.
- Hosts for both formats (dlopen/LoadLibrary, macOS bundles with a real
  CFBundleRef, split controllers with IConnectionPoint).
- Example plugins (gain/drive/pan effect; 16-voice subtractive synth).
- tools/check-plugins.sh: static-std release builds, .clap/.vst3 bundles,
  Rust hosts on the built files, C and C++ hosts compiled against the
  official headers, an isolated subnormal-input benchmark, and with
  --full clap-validator and Steinberg's VST3 validator. Results here:
  VST3 validator 537/537 for both plugins; clap-validator 33/33 and
  35/35 (one timing heuristic warning that the isolated benchmark shows
  is the validator's own subnormal generation).
- CI workflow running all of it on Linux x86-64/ARM, macOS and Windows.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
